WEBVTT

NOTE
This file was generated by Descript <www.descript.com>

00:00:01.100 --> 00:00:04.340
Leah: Learning to cope with stress can
require some trial and error and what

00:00:04.340 --> 00:00:06.290
works for one, might not work for another.

00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:10.430
Childhood stress can be present in
any setting that requires the child

00:00:10.450 --> 00:00:12.499
or young person to adapt or change.

00:00:13.190 --> 00:00:16.040
Lots of children and young
people face ongoing challenges

00:00:16.040 --> 00:00:19.829
they can’t quickly resolve, fear
kicks in, they get overwhelmed,

00:00:19.869 --> 00:00:21.459
and they try to escape from it.

00:00:22.159 --> 00:00:25.280
Rhiannon: Bad stress happens when
a threatening situation goes on and

00:00:25.280 --> 00:00:28.840
on, and that can erode confidence
and may seem overwhelming.

00:00:29.309 --> 00:00:32.570
The level of stress they feel
depends on past experience.

00:00:33.330 --> 00:00:36.720
Leah: Not all stress is bad
however, there’s ‘good stress’, too.

00:00:37.450 --> 00:00:40.370
Good stress is the type of stress
that accompanies excitement.

00:00:40.810 --> 00:00:44.150
The pulse quickens and adrenaline
increases, even though there is no

00:00:44.150 --> 00:00:47.559
threat or fear such as experienced
when riding a roller coaster.

00:00:48.330 --> 00:00:52.950
Rhiannon: Stress may be caused by positive
changes, such as starting a new activity.

00:00:53.489 --> 00:00:57.540
Stress is more likely to result in
positive outcomes if it is seen as a

00:00:57.540 --> 00:01:01.880
challenge or something to strive to
overcome and therefore can help motivate.

00:01:01.910 --> 00:01:06.580
When a child or young person confronts
a challenging situation and they feel

00:01:06.580 --> 00:01:10.569
they can manage or control it, it gives
them confidence and makes them stronger,

00:01:10.820 --> 00:01:12.510
ready to take on new challenges.

00:01:13.410 --> 00:01:16.869
Leah: Ongoing challenges and fears can
create stress in children and young

00:01:16.870 --> 00:01:21.500
people, but remembering past successes,
can help a child feel more in control.

00:01:21.980 --> 00:01:24.819
This can help children and young
people rise to challenges such

00:01:24.819 --> 00:01:28.369
as in sport or resolve problems
and build confidence in life.

00:01:29.080 --> 00:01:32.250
Rhiannon: The body’s ability to deal
with stress can get the brain and

00:01:32.250 --> 00:01:36.340
body ready to solve problems, and
kick into ‘fight or flight’ mode.

00:01:36.860 --> 00:01:41.289
Dealing with danger quickly, can help
people to feel safe again, and when the

00:01:41.289 --> 00:01:45.630
problem is overcome, the brain ‘feels
good’ more confident and resilient.

00:01:46.680 --> 00:01:50.690
Leah: Confident and resilient individuals
see stress as a challenge that they can

00:01:50.710 --> 00:01:53.280
and will master, and maybe even enjoy.

00:01:53.640 --> 00:01:56.850
If they do, their success will
carry over to the next challenge.

00:01:57.450 --> 00:02:00.439
Rhiannon: When children and young
people have support, and learn how to

00:02:00.440 --> 00:02:04.010
support themselves when handling stress,
they’re better equipped to handle

00:02:04.029 --> 00:02:05.809
challenges life may throw at them.

00:02:06.440 --> 00:02:10.479
The more success they have, the more
confident and in-control they’ll feel,

00:02:10.949 --> 00:02:14.799
then when they are feeling that success,
they are less likely to experience

00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:20.990
and dwell on the bad forms of stress.

